To implement the requested functionality, I have set up a complete flow including a mock data layer, a Server Action for creating posts, and a UI to display and add posts.

### 1. Data Layer (`lib/posts.ts`)
I created a simple file-based mock database using `posts.json`. The `getPosts` function is wrapped with `unstable_cache` and tagged with `'posts'` to simulate the caching requirement.

### 2. Server Action (`app/actions.ts`)
I implemented the `createPost` Server Action. Crucially, I used `updateTag('posts')` from `next/cache`. 
*   **Why `updateTag`?** In modern Next.js (especially Server Actions), `updateTag` provides "read-your-own-writes" semantics. This ensures that immediately after the action completes, the specific cache tag is invalidated, guaranteeing the user sees fresh data on the very next render, fulfilling your strict requirement against stale data.

### 3. UI (`app/page.tsx`)
I updated the main page to display the list of posts (fetched via the cached function) and provided a form that invokes the `createPost` Server Action.

### Files Created/Modified:

**`lib/posts.ts`** (Mock Database & Cached Fetching)
```typescript
import fs from 'fs/promises';
import path from 'path';
import { unstable_cache } from 'next/cache';

const DB_PATH = path.join(process.cwd(), 'posts.json');

// ... helper functions ...

// Cached version of getPosts using cache tags
export const getPosts = unstable_cache(
  async () => {
    return await readDb();
  },
  ['posts-data'], 
  { tags: ['posts'] } // The critical cache tag
);

export async function addPost(title: string) {
  // ... adds post to JSON file ...
}
```

**`app/actions.ts`** (Server Action)
```typescript
'use server';

import { updateTag } from 'next/cache';
import { addPost } from '@/lib/posts';

export async function createPost(formData: FormData) {
  const title = formData.get('title') as string;

  if (!title) return;

  await addPost(title);

  // INVALIDATION: updateTag ensures strict freshness for the next render
  updateTag('posts');
}
```

**`app/page.tsx`** (UI)
```typescript
import { getPosts } from '@/lib/posts';
import { createPost } from '@/app/actions';

export default async function Home() {
  const posts = await getPosts();

  return (
    <main style={{ padding: '20px' }}>
      <h1>Posts</h1>
      <form action={createPost}>
        <input name="title" type="text" required placeholder="New post title" />
        <button type="submit">Create Post</button>
      </form>
      {/* List of posts */}
      <ul>
        {posts.map((post) => (
          <li key={post.id}>{post.title}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </main>
  );
}
```

